commit = 3befc3e85ca1f2c602f603f6f610d518c44530fd
# The exact `wolf --version` string that commit produces (first line; the
# binary also prints a second line naming its lupin pairing — 0.1.23 at this
# pin). `lspconf doctor` fails on a mismatch: a stale local `wolf` producing
# green transcripts is the failure mode the pin exists to prevent.
#
# D57 decides the shape. A build is a release build only when the builder
# stamps BOTH `WOLF_COMMIT=<short sha>` and `WOLF_RELEASE=v{crate version}`,
# and the second is granted only when that exact tag points at HEAD. This pin
# is at v0.2.3, so a release build prints the BARE version and the pin clause.
# An unstamped build still prints `+dev.unknown` — the string any trunk build
# of the same crate version prints, which is exactly the stale-binary hole —
# so the pinned string remains a stamped build's, and `cargo xtask dist` in
# the submodule is the builder that computes the stamp (README §Running the
# server lane locally; its plain-cargo recipe cannot grant the release half).
#
# `xtask compat`'s `version_number` reads the number out of this string; it
# learned semver build metadata at le03 and takes the bare tag form
# unchanged — `0.2.3`, no `+` to strip. Asserted in xtask/src/compat.rs.
#
# SEVEN HEX DIGITS AGAIN, AND THIS BUMP IS THE CLEANEST EVIDENCE YET FOR
# wolf-lang#199. le05 measured the mechanism: `git rev-parse --short` sizes its
# AUTO abbreviation to the object count of the clone it runs in, so the width
# is a property of the BUILDER'S CLONE and not of the commit. le05 could only
# show that with arithmetic. le06 shows it with two clones on ONE BOX, at ONE
# COMMIT, disagreeing:
#
#   * the binary installed at ~/.local/bin/wolf prints `pin 3befc3e` — SEVEN;
#   * `git rev-parse --short 3befc3e8…` in THIS repo's `upstream/` submodule
#     (22,590 objects, measured 2026-09-02) answers `3befc3e8` — EIGHT.
#
# So a developer who follows vendor/README.md's own pin-bump ritual — build the
# submodule, read `wolf --version` — gets a string this file would have to
# record as eight digits, and a developer who installs the published archive
# gets seven, and `doctor` can only accept one of them. Nothing about the
# commit changed between those two readings. The line below records what the
# ACQUIRED artifact prints, because that is what CI acquires and what a user
# runs; a locally built submodule binary on a large clone will fail `doctor`
# here, and that refusal is the pin doing its job, not a bug in this file.
#
# The defect is upstream, in D57's clause being clone-dependent at all, and it
# stays filed as wolf-lang#199 — see docs/COMPAT.md.
version = "wolf 0.2.3 (wolfgang, pin 3befc3e)"
